+/* DirectSound's mixbins (XDK 5xxx DSMIXBIN_*): 0 front left, 1 front right,
+ * 2 centre, 3 LFE, 4 back left, 5 back right, 6 to 9 the crosstalk submixes
+ * of 3D voices (front left/right, back left/right), 10 the I3DL2 reverb
+ * send, 11 to 30 effect sends (FXSEND_0..19): the inputs of the effect
+ * chains in the title's GP image, which mix their outputs to the speakers.
+ *
+ * The parity sum (=1) treated every send as a speaker of the side its
+ * number fell on. Project Gotham Racing 2 drives sends harder than the dry
+ * pair: in a race each engine and effect voice goes to the front pair at
+ * its pan and to FXSEND 1..3 (bins 12-14) 6-10 dB louder; in car selection
+ * the music's front pair is muted and it plays through bin 24 alone (both
+ * channels summed into one send, so the send is mono). By parity all of
+ * bins 12, 14 and 24 landed on the left: the front end was 13 dB louder on
+ * the left and a race was almost entirely on it, with the right carrying
+ * only bin 13.
+ *
+ * The GP is a stub, so what an effect chain does (EQ, compression, its own
+ * pan) is unknown here. This takes its output to be its input, centred:
+ * every effect send goes to both sides at equal power. The reverb send
+ * carries a dry copy, so it goes in at half that; the crosstalk bins keep
+ * their sides (Jet Set Radio Future's 3D voices, bins 6 to 10, were why the
+ * parity sum was written). */
